### Viral infections of the skin

### Respiratory viral infections

### Viral infections with neurological involvement

1.  Japanese B encephalitis

    - **Definition** - a flavivirus, most important cause of endemic encephalitis in Japan, China, Russia, SE Asia, India and Pakistan
    - **Epidemiology:**
      - <u>Incidence</u> - 10000-20000 cases reported worldwide
    - **Virology of JEV:**
      - <u>Reservoir</u> - pigs and aquatic birds are the virus reservoirs
      - <u>Transmission</u> - by mosquitoes (exposure to rice paddies is a recognised risk factor)
      - <u>Incubation</u> - 4-21d
    - **Clinical features of JEV** - most are subclinical especially in childhood, but those manifesting w/ neurological disease (1%) have high mortality (25%):
      - **Systemic illness** - fever, malaise, and anorexia occurs in the initial systemic illness
      - **Encephalitis** - typically affects brainstem functions:
        - Reduced GC
        - Cranial nerve palsies (including respiratory and cardiovascular centre functioning)
        - Flaccid or spastic paralysis
        - Seizures
        - Extrapyramidal features
      - **Neurological features other than encephalitis** - meningism, photophobia, headache etc.
    - **Ix:**
      - **Routine bloods** - CBC, RFT:
        - <u>CBC</u> - neutrophilia
        - <u>RFT</u> - hypoNa
      - **LP** - typically lymphocytosis w/ elevated protein (+/- CSF Ag detection)
    - **Mx:**
      - **Principles of Mx:**
        - <u>Supportive care</u> - monitoring and Tx of complications
        - <u>Preventative care</u> - vaccincation for travellers to endemic areas during monsoon periods (+/- childhood vacination in endemic regions)

2.  West Nile virus

    - **Definition** - a flavivirus, an important cause of neurological disease in endemic areas
    - **Virology of West Nile virus:**
      - <u>Endemic regions</u> - along the West Nile extending from Australia, India, Russia through Africa, Southern Europe and across to North America
      - <u>Reservoir</u> - avian
      - <u>Transmission</u> - mosquito vector
      - <u>Incubation</u> - 2-6d
    - **Clinical features of West Nile virus infection** - most infections are asymptomatic:
      - <u>Mild systemic illness</u> (most common presentation) - febrile illness and arthralgia (+/- a maculopapular rash)
      - <u>Neurological illness</u> (1% but a/w 10% mortality) - encephalitis, meningitis, or asymmetrical flaccid paralysis
    - **Ix** - detection of viral RNA in blood or CSF
    - **Mx** - supportive

3.  Enterovirus 71

4.  Nipah virus encephalitis

5.  Human T-cell lymphotropic virus type I

